WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.819
Module 5.

00:02.169 --> 00:04.793
The verification protocol and the review record.

00:05.143 --> 00:08.579
Verification is the most important control in this programme.

00:08.929 --> 00:12.428
This module sets out what to check, how to check it, how

00:12.428 --> 00:15.927
deep the review must go for different kinds of work, and how

00:15.927 --> 00:19.135
to record the review so that it can be proved later.

00:20.885 --> 00:24.790
By the end of this module you will be able to:

00:24.790 --> 00:28.696
apply the five verification checks to any AI output; choose the

00:28.696 --> 00:32.601
right review tier for the task; spot the red flags of

00:32.601 --> 00:36.152
fabricated authority; create a contemporaneous review record and control versions.

00:37.902 --> 00:40.273
This is the most important module in the

00:40.273 --> 00:42.348
programme, and it rests on one rule.

00:42.698 --> 00:44.997
Verify against the primary source.

00:45.347 --> 00:47.936
Never ask the AI to verify itself.

00:48.286 --> 00:51.092
When you ask a chatbot whether the case it just

00:51.092 --> 00:53.899
gave you is real, it may simply confirm it, because

00:53.899 --> 00:56.425
it is running the same pattern-matching a second time.

00:56.775 --> 01:00.124
Verification means a human opening the actual source in

01:00.124 --> 01:03.474
an authoritative database or official record and reading it.

01:05.223 --> 01:09.194
Every AI output that will be relied on gets five checks.

01:09.544 --> 01:16.173
One, existence: every case, statute, rule and regulation is found in an authoritative source.

01:16.523 --> 01:20.920
Two, accuracy: every quotation matches the source word for word, and

01:20.920 --> 01:24.917
every pinpoint citation points to the right page or paragraph.

01:25.267 --> 01:29.900
Three, proposition: the source actually supports the point it is cited for.

01:30.250 --> 01:33.036
This is the check that catches content errors.

01:33.386 --> 01:36.709
Four, currency: the authority is still good law, so run

01:36.709 --> 01:40.032
it through a citator, confirm the statute is the current

01:40.032 --> 01:43.022
version and confirm it is from the right jurisdiction.

01:43.372 --> 01:48.842
And five, facts and figures: every date, deadline, number and factual statement

01:48.842 --> 01:53.856
is checked against the client record, and calculations are redone independently.

01:55.606 --> 02:00.331
Not every output needs the same depth of review, so we use three tiers.

02:00.681 --> 02:04.536
Tier one is internal working material: notes, brainstorming and

02:04.536 --> 02:08.390
internal summaries that nobody outside the firm relies on.

02:08.740 --> 02:11.457
The author checks before anything is reused.

02:11.807 --> 02:15.760
Tier two is client-facing advice: advice, letters, contract drafts

02:15.760 --> 02:19.713
and anything a client or counterparty will rely on.

02:20.063 --> 02:22.992
That requires the full five checks by the

02:22.992 --> 02:25.555
author, plus sign-off by a supervising lawyer.

02:25.905 --> 02:29.063
Tier three covers filings with a court, tribunal

02:29.063 --> 02:32.221
or regulator, and formal opinions or closing letters.

02:32.571 --> 02:36.214
That requires the full five checks, a documented cite-check, personal

02:36.214 --> 02:39.857
confirmation of the review by the lawyer who signs, and

02:39.857 --> 02:43.136
disclosure of AI use where the forum requires it.

02:44.886 --> 02:47.777
Learn the red flags of fabricated authority.

02:48.127 --> 02:51.929
Be suspicious of anything too perfect: a case that fits your

02:51.929 --> 02:55.731
argument exactly, with a quotation that reads like your own brief.

02:56.081 --> 03:00.087
Be suspicious of anything you can't find: the case is not in

03:00.087 --> 03:04.092
your research database, or the citation leads to a completely different case.

03:04.442 --> 03:08.442
Watch for odd details: reporter volumes or page numbers that don't fit

03:08.442 --> 03:12.441
the year, courts that don't exist, or party names that don't match.

03:12.791 --> 03:15.969
And watch for a holding with no pinpoint, or a

03:15.969 --> 03:18.828
quotation that you cannot locate anywhere in the opinion.

03:19.178 --> 03:23.509
Any one of these means stop and investigate before the document goes anywhere.

03:25.259 --> 03:26.722
Now the review record.

03:27.072 --> 03:29.591
If it isn't recorded, it didn't happen.

03:29.941 --> 03:32.977
When something goes wrong, the firm will need to show that

03:32.977 --> 03:36.013
a proper review took place before the work was relied on.

03:36.363 --> 03:39.068
A memory of having checked is not evidence.

03:39.418 --> 03:43.651
So for every tier two and tier three output, record the matter

03:43.651 --> 03:47.883
and the document, the exact version reviewed, the AI tool used and

03:47.883 --> 03:52.116
whether it was an enterprise or consumer plan, who reviewed it and

03:52.116 --> 03:56.349
in what role, the date and time preferably as a system timestamp

03:56.349 --> 04:00.581
or sign-off in your matter or document-management system, which checks were performed

04:00.581 --> 04:04.461
and against which sources, and what issues were found and fixed.

04:04.811 --> 04:06.436
Make the record at the time.

04:06.786 --> 04:08.934
A record reconstructed after a complaint is worth

04:08.934 --> 04:11.082
far less than one made in the moment.

04:12.832 --> 04:14.771
Finally, version control.

04:15.121 --> 04:18.343
First, save the raw AI output exactly as

04:18.343 --> 04:21.564
generated, together with the prompt and response log.

04:21.914 --> 04:26.512
Second, review a fixed version, and record which version number you checked.

04:26.862 --> 04:30.829
Third, re-review every later edit: any change made after the review, by

04:30.829 --> 04:34.466
anyone, gets its own check before the work is relied on.

04:34.816 --> 04:38.331
And fourth, treat re-use as new work: reusing an output

04:38.331 --> 04:41.846
on another matter, for a different client, or after the

04:41.846 --> 04:45.010
facts or law have changed, requires a fresh review.

04:45.360 --> 04:48.299
These four habits are what allow a firm to prove

04:48.299 --> 04:50.944
later exactly what was checked, by whom, and when.

04:52.694 --> 04:54.273
Let's recap the key points.

04:54.623 --> 04:59.337
Verify against primary sources; never ask the AI to check itself.

04:59.687 --> 05:05.631
Five checks: existence, accuracy, proposition, currency, facts and figures.

05:05.981 --> 05:10.161
Match review depth to the tier; filings get a documented cite-check.

05:10.511 --> 05:13.854
Record the review at the time and re-review any later edit.

05:15.604 --> 05:16.707
Knowledge check.

05:17.057 --> 05:19.768
You ask the same AI tool, 'Is the

05:19.768 --> 05:22.479
case you cited real?' and it replies 'Yes'.

05:22.829 --> 05:24.269
What have you verified?

05:24.619 --> 05:26.709
Option A: The case exists.

05:27.059 --> 05:31.343
Option B: Nothing: the AI cannot verify itself.

05:31.693 --> 05:35.930
Option C: The case exists, but not that it is good law.

05:36.280 --> 05:39.078
Take a moment to decide before the answer appears.

05:44.828 --> 05:46.512
The answer is option B.

05:46.862 --> 05:50.263
Self-verification is the same pattern-matching run twice.

05:50.613 --> 05:52.796
Only a primary source check counts.
